      {
        type: "application",
        title: "Clinical Application: The Confused Elder",
        content: `**Scenario**: Mrs. Chen, 78, admitted post-hip ORIF. POD#2, night shift reports she's pulling at her IV, calling for her late husband, doesn't know she's in hospital. She was alert at admission.

**Your assessment**:
• **CAM (Confusion Assessment Method)**: acute onset ✓ + inattention ✓ + disorganized thinking ✓ → **DELIRIUM**
• Review meds: received **hydromorphone PCA + diphenhydramine** (anticholinergic) for sleep → **culprits**
• VS: T 100.4°F, UA pending — possible UTI brewing
• O2 sat 91% on RA — hypoxia contributing

**Interventions (priority order)**:
1. **O2 supplementation** to >94% (treat hypoxia)
2. **D/C diphenhydramine**, taper opioid, send UA
3. **Reorient gently**, photo of family at bedside, glasses on
4. **Avoid restraints** — bed alarm, 1:1 sitter if available
5. **Preserve sleep**: lights off 10pm, cluster care, no vitals 0200 if stable
6. **Document with CAM** every shift — track trajectory

**Family education**: "Confusion is from her medical condition, not permanent — we expect improvement as we fix the cause."`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="34" font-weight="bold">Mrs. Chen — Post-Op Delirium</text><text x="550" y="100" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">78yo POD#2 hip ORIF, acute confusion</text><rect x="60" y="140" width="500" height="380" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="3"/><text x="310" y="180" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">The Culprits</text><text x="80" y="225" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Hydromorphone</tspan> PCA (opioid)</text><text x="80" y="260" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Diphenhydramine</tspan> for sleep</text><text x="120" y="290" fill="#888" font-size="16">(anticholinergic — never in elderly!)</text><text x="80" y="325" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Hypoxia</tspan> SpO2 91%</text><text x="80" y="360" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Possible UTI</tspan> (T 100.4°)</text><text x="80" y="395" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• Sleep deprivation, unfamiliar room</text><text x="80" y="430" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• Pain (under or over-treated)</text><text x="80" y="475" fill="#10b981" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">→ Multifactorial = typical</text><rect x="580" y="140" width="460" height="380" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="3"/><text x="810" y="180" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">Interventions (priority)</text><text x="600" y="225" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">1. <tspan fill="#0ea5e9">O2</tspan> to &gt;94%</text><text x="600" y="260" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">2. <tspan fill="#0ea5e9">D/C diphenhydramine</tspan></text><text x="600" y="295" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">3. <tspan fill="#0ea5e9">Taper opioid</tspan>, scheduled APAP</text><text x="600" y="330" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">4. <tspan fill="#0ea5e9">Send UA + cultures</tspan></text><text x="600" y="365" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">5. <tspan fill="#0ea5e9">Reorient</tspan> + family photo</text><text x="600" y="400" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">6. <tspan fill="#0ea5e9">Glasses/hearing aids</tspan> ON</text><text x="600" y="435" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">7. <tspan fill="#0ea5e9">Bed alarm</tspan>, NO restraints</text><text x="600" y="475" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">CAM q-shift to track</text><rect x="60" y="560" width="980" height="220" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="600" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">Family Education Script</text><text x="550" y="650" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20" font-style="italic">"Mom's confusion is from her surgery, medications,</text><text x="550" y="680" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20" font-style="italic">and a possible infection — not a stroke or dementia.</text><text x="550" y="710" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20" font-style="italic">As we fix the causes, her thinking should clear over</text><text x="550" y="740" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20" font-style="italic">days. Your presence and familiar voice help a lot."</text><rect x="60" y="820" width="980" height="240" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="860" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">CAM Criteria (Delirium = 1+2 AND 3 or 4)</text><text x="100" y="905" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">1. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Acute onset</tspan> + fluctuating course</text><text x="100" y="940" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">2. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Inattention</tspan> (can't count back from 20)</text><text x="100" y="975" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">3. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Disorganized thinking</tspan> (rambling, illogical)</text><text x="100" y="1010" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">4. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Altered consciousness</tspan> (drowsy or hyperalert)</text><text x="550" y="1050" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Document CAM each shift — trajectory matters</text></svg>`,
          caption: "Multifactorial delirium — peel back each cause, expect gradual improvement."
        }
      }
