      {
        type: "application",
        title: "Clinical Application: SUD Records Request",
        content: `**Scenario**: You're a charge nurse on inpatient psych. The patient's primary care physician calls: "I'm seeing John Doe next week, can you fax over his discharge summary including his meth use treatment?" John signed standard HIPAA consent at admission.

**Analysis**:
• **Standard HIPAA consent** covers **TPO** (Treatment, Payment, Operations) for **general medical info**
• But John's records contain **substance use disorder treatment** information → falls under **42 CFR Part 2**
• 42 CFR Part 2 requires **specific, separate written consent** to disclose SUD info — even to **other treating providers**
• The standard HIPAA form is **NOT sufficient**

**Correct action**:
1. **Do NOT release** SUD records yet
2. **Inform PCP** politely: "Federal law requires a specific Part 2 release for SUD information — we'll need to obtain John's written consent on the Part 2 form."
3. **Approach John** with the Part 2 consent form, explain what will be shared, and obtain signature
4. **Document** the discussion and consent
5. **Release records only after** signed Part 2 consent

**Exceptions** to Part 2 (no consent needed): medical emergency, court order, child abuse reporting, research with IRB protections.

**Why so strict?** SUD records can lead to discrimination, loss of employment, custody loss — extra protection encourages people to seek treatment.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="32" font-weight="bold">42 CFR Part 2 in Action</text><text x="550" y="100" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">SUD records get HIGHEST protection</text><rect x="60" y="140" width="490" height="380" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="3"/><text x="305" y="180" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">DO NOT Release</text><text x="80" y="225" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">Standard HIPAA consent is</text><text x="80" y="255" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20"><tspan fill="#ef4444" font-weight="bold">NOT enough</tspan> for SUD records.</text><text x="80" y="305" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">Even to:</text><text x="80" y="340" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• Primary care physician</text><text x="80" y="370" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• Other hospitals</text><text x="80" y="400" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• Family members</text><text x="80" y="430" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• Employers</text><text x="80" y="460" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">• Even mom asking on phone</text><text x="80" y="500" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">Without Part 2 consent</text><rect x="570" y="140" width="470" height="380" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="3"/><text x="805" y="180" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">Correct Steps</text><text x="590" y="225" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">1. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Recognize</tspan> Part 2 applies</text><text x="590" y="265" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">2. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Inform PCP</tspan> of legal need</text><text x="590" y="305" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">3. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Approach patient</tspan> with</text><text x="630" y="335" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">Part 2 consent form</text><text x="590" y="375" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">4. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Explain</tspan> what's disclosed</text><text x="590" y="415" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">5. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Obtain signed</tspan> consent</text><text x="590" y="455" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">6. <tspan fill="#ffd700">Document</tspan> and release</text><text x="805" y="500" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">Then it's lawful</text><rect x="60" y="560" width="980" height="220" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="600" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">Part 2 Exceptions (no consent needed)</text><text x="80" y="645" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Medical emergency</tspan> — treat the unconscious overdose</text><text x="80" y="680" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Court order</tspan> — judge-issued, specific to records</text><text x="80" y="715" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Child abuse reporting</tspan> — mandatory reporters</text><text x="80" y="750" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">• <tspan fill="#ffd700">Research</tspan> with IRB protections</text><rect x="60" y="820" width="980" height="240" rx="15" fill="none" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="860" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">Why So Strict?</text><text x="550" y="905" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">SUD information can lead to:</text><text x="550" y="945" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">discrimination • employment loss • custody loss</text><text x="550" y="985" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">housing denial • criminal prosecution</text><text x="550" y="1030" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Extra privacy → patients seek treatment</text></svg>`,
          caption: "When in doubt about SUD records, get the Part 2 form signed — TPO sharing doesn't apply."
        }
      }
