      {
        type: "application",
        title: "Putting It Together: A Charge-Nurse Mindset",
        content: `When you sit down for NCLEX, switch into charge-nurse mindset for any item containing the words "delegate," "assign," "most appropriate for the LPN," or "which task can be done by the UAP." Run this 30-second mental script:

Step 1: Read the stem twice. Identify who is being assigned what, and to whom.

Step 2: For each option, ask: what is the most acute task in this option? If it requires assessment, planning, evaluation, teaching of new content, IV push, blood, titration, or an unstable patient — the answer is RN-only, regardless of who is being assigned.

Step 3: For LPN options, confirm: stable patient, predictable outcome, expected response. LPNs can administer oral and IM meds, do sterile dressing changes, insert Foleys, suction trachs, and reinforce teaching. They cannot push IV meds, do initial assessments, manage transfusions, or take care of unpredictable patients.

Step 4: For UAP options, confirm: no assessment, no teaching, no meds, no interpretation. Vitals must be on a stable patient. Specimens must be non-sterile. Ambulation must be a known-tolerated activity.

Step 5: If you are torn between two options, pick the one where the work flows DOWN the pyramid most cleanly — the most stable patient and most routine task to the lowest-licensed competent person, and the RN keeps the most acute patient.

Common traps to memorize: (1) "First-dose" or "first time" anything = RN. (2) "New admission" = RN initial assessment. (3) "Newly diagnosed" + teaching = RN. (4) "Unstable" "deteriorating" "post-op day 0" "PACU" = RN. (5) "Just got out of orientation" or "float pool" describing a UAP = competency question — be cautious about delegating anything complex. (6) Reinforcing teaching is LPN-okay; initial teaching is RN-only. (7) Blood products = RN. (8) Critical lab call-back = RN. Practice 20 delegation items, walk all 5 Rights every time, and watch your accuracy climb above 85%.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="38" font-weight="bold">Charge-Nurse 30-Second Script</text><rect x="80" y="110" width="940" height="100" fill="#6366f1" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="150" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">STEP 1 — Read stem twice</text><text x="100" y="185" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">Identify who is being assigned what, and to whom.</text><rect x="80" y="225" width="940" height="100" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="265" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">STEP 2 — Find the most acute task in each option</text><text x="100" y="300" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">If assess / teach / IV push / blood / unstable — answer is RN-only.</text><rect x="80" y="340" width="940" height="100" fill="#10b981" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="380" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">STEP 3 — LPN check: stable + predictable?</text><text x="100" y="415" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">PO/IM meds, sterile procedures, reinforce teaching — yes. IV push — no.</text><rect x="80" y="455" width="940" height="100" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="495" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">STEP 4 — UAP check: no assess, no teach, no meds</text><text x="100" y="530" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="18">Tasks only, on stable patients with predictable outcomes.</text><rect x="80" y="570" width="940" height="100" fill="#ec4899" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="610" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">STEP 5 — Flow work DOWN the pyramid</text><text x="100" y="645" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">Most acute stays with RN. Most routine goes to lowest competent level.</text><rect x="80" y="700" width="940" height="350" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="3" rx="10"/><text x="550" y="740" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">8 Trap Phrases = RN-Only</text><text x="120" y="785" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">1. "First dose" / "first time"</text><text x="120" y="815" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">2. "New admission"</text><text x="120" y="845" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">3. "Newly diagnosed" + teaching</text><text x="120" y="875" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">4. "Unstable" / "deteriorating"</text><text x="600" y="785" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">5. "Post-op day 0" / "PACU"</text><text x="600" y="815" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">6. Blood / transfusion</text><text x="600" y="845" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">7. Titrated drip</text><text x="600" y="875" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">8. Critical lab call-back</text><text x="550" y="940" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-style="italic">When in doubt: keep it.</text><text x="550" y="990" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-style="italic">The RN's accountability never delegates.</text></svg>`,
          caption: "30-second charge-nurse script plus the 8 most common RN-only trap phrases."
        }
      }
