        explanation: "Active pulmonary tuberculosis requires AIRBORNE precautions: N95 respirator (fit-tested), negative-pressure room (also called Airborne Infection Isolation Room or AIIR), and the door must remain closed except for entry and exit. Remember the airborne mnemonic MTV: Measles, Tuberculosis, Varicella. C. difficile requires CONTACT precautions (gown + gloves) AND soap-and-water hand hygiene because alcohol-based gels do not kill C. diff spores. RSV is also CONTACT precautions, not airborne. Bacterial meningitis (typically N. meningitidis) is DROPLET precautions — surgical mask within 3-6 feet, NOT an N95 — and the room does not need to be negative-pressure. This is one of the most consistently tested distinctions on the NCLEX: do not let the word 'meningitis' trigger an airborne reflex. The category is determined by the size of the infectious particle: airborne droplets less than 5 microns stay suspended in air (requires N95 and negative pressure), while droplet pathogens are larger than 5 microns, fall within 3-6 feet, and require only a surgical mask.",
