        type: "application",
        title: "Your Capstone Plan — 24 Hours Before, and After",
        content: `THE 24-HOUR-BEFORE CHECKLIST (do this Sunday for a Monday exam)

Morning (8 am - noon):
1. Wake naturally, no alarm if possible
2. Light breakfast, hydrate
3. Walk outside 20-30 minutes
4. One final 30-minute glance at cheat sheet — STOP at noon
5. Confirm test center address + parking in GPS

Afternoon (noon - 6 pm):
1. NO studying — this is non-negotiable
2. Light lunch with a non-nursing person if possible
3. Movie, walk, hobby, anything restorative
4. Lay out clothes (layers, no hood, comfortable shoes)
5. Pack bag: 2 IDs (matching ATT exactly), printed ATT, sweater, water bottle, granola bar
6. Confirm alarm clock + backup alarm

Evening (6 pm - bed):
1. Normal dinner — protein + complex carbs, NOT a heavy meal
2. Light wind-down activity — reading, music, gentle conversation
3. No phone after 9 pm if possible (blue light disrupts sleep onset)
4. Bed by 10 pm
5. If you cannot sleep, do not stress — keep eyes closed, do box breathing, count breaths. Eyes-closed rest delivers 60-70% of sleep's restorative benefit.

THE POST-EXAM 48 HOURS (regardless of outcome)

Immediately after: Do not debrief in the lobby. Other candidates often misremember questions and create false doubts. Walk to your car, drive home or to a quiet spot, eat a real meal, sleep if you can.

Quick Results purchase (T+48 hours): For $7.95 on Pearson VUE's site, you get an unofficial pass/fail in 48 hours. Official results from your state board come in 2-6 weeks.

IF YOU PASS: Send a thank-you note to one person who supported you during prep. Save your study materials for at least 1 year — you may help a colleague. Begin onboarding paperwork for your nursing role. Take 1-2 weeks of mental rest before starting orientation; new-nurse cognitive load is heavy and you have earned a buffer.

IF YOU FAIL: First — failing the NCLEX is not a verdict on your worth as a nurse. The national first-time pass rate is ~88%, meaning ~12% of capable nurses re-test. NCSBN allows retesting after 45 days. Use the Candidate Performance Report (CPR) from your state board — it identifies which content areas were below standard. Build a focused 6-week study plan targeting only those areas. Most retest passes happen with ~50% less total study time than the original attempt because the gaps are now known.

Either way: this exam is one day in a 40-year career. It is high-stakes, but it is not your identity. You are a nurse the moment your patient needs you — license or not.

You have a plan. Now go execute.`,
