        type: "intro",
        title: "Mock Exam 6: Skin, Bones & Hormones",
        content: `Welcome to your sixth full-length Computer Adaptive Test (CAT). This 75-item simulation concentrates on three integrated body systems that historically generate high-yield NCLEX items: integumentary (burns, pressure injuries, skin cancers), musculoskeletal (fractures, compartment syndrome, post-op joint replacement), and endocrine (diabetes, thyroid, adrenal, pituitary disorders).

These three systems are interwoven on the actual exam because they share assessment frameworks (perfusion, tissue integrity, hormonal regulation) and overlapping interventions (fluid resuscitation, glucose control, neurovascular checks). Mastery here also pulls forward Mock Exam 4 critical care skills (Parkland for burns, DKA management) and Mock Exam 5 prioritization (which post-op orthopedic patient do you see first?).

Test conditions for this CAT: 75 questions, 90-minute time cap, no calculator help except the on-screen NCLEX calculator, no skipping. Use the testing environment from Mock 1: quiet room, water, ID card, single bathroom break window. Track time on every 25-item block (aim for 25 items in 30 minutes).

After completion, run the Mock Exam Debrief Methodology from Lesson 6: error-type tagging (knowledge gap vs. test-taking error vs. careless), high-yield review list, and a 24-hour re-test on missed concepts. This lesson previews the five most tested concept clusters and gives you a representative NGN case to anchor your studying.

Goal score: 65% or higher with strong performance on burn calculations, neurovascular assessment, and endocrine medication safety.`,
