        type: "application",
        title: "Your NGN Drill Protocol for Exam Day",
        content: `For the 10-15% of items that will be NGN on your actual exam, run this fixed protocol every time:

**Step 1 — Identify the item type within 2 seconds.** The visual layout tells you immediately: highlight has a passage with selectable text spans; drag-and-drop has a list of tiles or a sequence ladder; drop-down cloze has paragraph with embedded selectable menus; matrix has rows-by-columns checkboxes; bowtie has the distinctive five-region map; extended multi-response looks like SATA but with 6-10 options instead of 4-6.

**Step 2 — Infer the rubric.** Highlight and extended multi-response use +1/-1. Matrix is all-or-nothing per row. Everything else is 0/+1 (safe).

**Step 3 — Apply confidence thresholds.**
- Rubric 1 (0/+1): Select if 30%+ confident. Never leave blank — there's literally no downside.
- Rubric 2 (+/-): Select only if 70%+ confident. When in doubt, leave it.
- Rubric 3 (matrix): Treat each row as a standalone question. Verify the entire row before moving on.

**Step 4 — Verify rationale, not gut.** For each selected option, you should be able to mentally cite a textbook reason ("crackles indicate fluid overload in CHF"). If you can't articulate the rationale, downgrade your confidence by one tier.

**Step 5 — Time budget.** Allocate ~3 minutes per NGN item (vs. ~1 min for traditional). A case study (6 linked items) gets ~6 minutes total because items 2-6 share context with item 1. Use the first item to fully absorb the chart; subsequent items move faster.

**Step 6 — Don't overthink case studies.** Each item in the bundle is scored independently. Getting item 2 wrong does NOT propagate to item 3. Treat them as 6 separate questions that happen to share a patient.

**Career-relevance bonus:** This same Clinical Judgment Measurement Model (CJMM) framework will appear in your hospital's annual competency checkoffs and Magnet-recognition skill validations. Mastering NGN reasoning isn't just NCLEX prep — it's the operating system of modern nursing practice. Per NCSBN's 2024 graduate-nurse outcomes study, candidates who scored in the top quartile on NGN items had 31% fewer first-year medication errors than the bottom quartile.`,
