        title: "Final Exhaustion Drill — 100 Questions, 2 Hours",
        content: `Today's mock is the most important practice exam you will take: 100 mixed-topic questions completed in exactly 2 hours (120 minutes), without breaks, simulating the cognitive and physical exhaustion of hour 4-5 of the real NCLEX. Real-exam logistics: Pearson VUE allots a maximum of 5 hours including two optional breaks (one programmed after item 60, one optional after item 120). Most candidates testing in the new CAT format finish in 75-145 items (the 2023 NCSBN minimum), with mean test length of 92 items and mean completion time of 3.1 hours. But you must train for the worst-case 5-hour grind because the CAT can extend you to 145 items if you're hovering at the cut-score.

The purpose of this drill is NOT to learn new content. By lesson 11 of book 10 your content is locked. The purpose is to build endurance, calibrate pacing, and stress-test your decision-making under fatigue. Research from cognitive load science (Sweller, 2024 meta-analysis) shows accuracy drops 18-22% in hours 3-4 of high-stakes testing, mostly due to working-memory depletion rather than knowledge loss. The fix is exposure — repeated practice at the edge of mental endurance trains your prefrontal cortex to sustain executive function longer.

This lesson gives you the protocol: how to set up your practice environment, how to pace 100 items in 120 minutes (72 seconds/item), how to handle fatigue mid-exam, when to use the "skip and return" function (spoiler: you can't — NCLEX is forward-only), and what to do in the last 10 items when you can feel your concentration crumbling. We'll also cover the official Pearson VUE pacing thresholds and what they signal about your CAT trajectory.

Treat this as a dress rehearsal. Phone off. Door closed. Water bottle. Snack permitted only during the simulated 10-minute break at item 60.`,
